00:09:44.677 --> 00:09:45.702
His opening salvo--

00:09:45.702 --> 00:09:47.836
You getting this confused
with health class.

00:09:47.836 --> 00:09:49.439
Like coach Bunch
No, no, no

00:09:49.439 --> 00:09:51.861
Teaching you about it
I specifically remember it.

00:09:51.861 --> 00:09:55.694
He told a story about a
man who did something,

00:09:58.612 --> 00:10:00.885
like had promiscuous sex

00:10:00.885 --> 00:10:02.679
or cheated on his wife or something.

00:10:02.679 --> 00:10:04.049
And it was just like,

00:10:04.049 --> 00:10:06.826
and that man got genital warts.

00:10:06.826 --> 00:10:07.717
And I was like, whoa,

00:10:07.717 --> 00:10:08.675
we're going here?

00:10:08.675 --> 00:10:10.047
Alright, okay.

00:10:10.047 --> 00:10:11.345
What does that mean?

00:10:11.345 --> 00:10:12.554
And then he was like,

00:10:12.554 --> 00:10:14.193
"And that man was me."

00:10:14.193 --> 00:10:15.244
And then it's like, whoa.

00:10:15.244 --> 00:10:16.077
What.
Which is you got

00:10:16.077 --> 00:10:18.050
genital warts.

00:10:18.050 --> 00:10:18.883
And so --

00:10:18.883 --> 00:10:20.055
I was not there for this.

00:10:20.055 --> 00:10:20.888
So okay.

00:10:20.888 --> 00:10:21.721
I would have remembered that.

00:10:21.721 --> 00:10:22.704
You would remembered that.

00:10:22.704 --> 00:10:24.577
You think he was like,

00:10:24.577 --> 00:10:26.966
"And genital warts are what you make them"

00:10:26.966 --> 00:10:27.799
What?

00:10:27.799 --> 00:10:30.010
No, because the way that they
did the fall retreats is--

00:10:30.010 --> 00:10:32.411
If you get the warts that is.

00:10:32.411 --> 00:10:33.244
It was a guy who--

00:10:33.244 --> 00:10:34.243
It's what you make it.
There was a guy,

00:10:34.243 --> 00:10:35.772
it was always a guy.

00:10:35.772 --> 00:10:38.189
Who spoke the entire weekend.

00:10:39.870 --> 00:10:42.136
And so his opening talk was genital warts.

00:10:42.136 --> 00:10:45.677
And he had us by the
genital warts at that point.

00:10:45.677 --> 00:10:47.639
It was like whatever this guy says,

00:10:47.639 --> 00:10:50.003
don't miss any of the main talks, right?

00:10:50.003 --> 00:10:51.922
"Cause he opened with genital warts.

00:10:51.922 --> 00:10:52.954
Mm-hmm.
And then he went to

00:10:52.954 --> 00:10:54.384
just talking about relationships

00:10:54.384 --> 00:10:55.321
and marriage or something,

00:10:55.321 --> 00:10:57.292
and I kind of swear he said,

00:10:57.292 --> 00:10:58.851
"It's what you make it."

00:10:58.851 --> 00:11:00.281
It didn't seem revolutionary at the time,

00:11:00.281 --> 00:11:02.182
and I don't necessarily think
that it is revolutionary,

00:11:02.182 --> 00:11:04.449
but the question was,

00:11:04.449 --> 00:11:06.185
that is impacted you the most.

00:11:06.185 --> 00:11:10.117
And I just find myself in
situations all the time,

00:11:10.117 --> 00:11:11.992
and I'm like,

00:11:11.992 --> 00:11:12.959
"It's what you make it."

00:11:14.373 --> 00:11:16.564
It has been helpful as a joke,

00:11:16.564 --> 00:11:18.030
but also as a reality.

00:11:18.030 --> 00:11:21.511
"Cause it helps you focus
on what you can control

00:11:21.511 --> 00:11:22.434
in any given situation.

00:11:22.434 --> 00:11:23.392
Yeah.

00:11:23.392 --> 00:11:25.667
As opposed to the parts that you can't.

00:11:25.667 --> 00:11:28.346
I think we'll come back to that.

00:11:28.346 --> 00:11:30.679
I think there's some advice,

00:11:31.684 --> 00:11:33.477
it's not just, is the advice good,

00:11:33.477 --> 00:11:35.644
but who is the advice for?

00:11:37.645 --> 00:11:41.101
The advice that has always
resonated with me is,

00:11:41.101 --> 00:11:44.100
"Anything worth doing
is worth doing right."

00:11:44.100 --> 00:11:46.861
But that's a bit problematic
for a guy like me

00:11:46.861 --> 00:11:51.499
that gets over focused on
the rightness of things

00:11:51.499 --> 00:11:53.423
and going about them in the right way--

00:11:53.423 --> 00:11:55.141
Cause you might not do
something if you don't think

00:11:55.141 --> 00:11:56.056
you can do it right.

00:11:56.056 --> 00:11:59.556
Yeah, there's some fear and trepidation,

00:12:00.461 --> 00:12:02.751
If that rings in my ears too much.

00:12:02.751 --> 00:12:05.251
And then it's never clear cut.

00:12:07.467 --> 00:12:09.085
I'd like to believe that it is,

00:12:09.085 --> 00:12:11.028
and say okay, this is perfected,

00:12:11.028 --> 00:12:12.195
I can move on.

00:12:13.763 --> 00:12:16.680
You gotta balance all these things.

00:12:17.570 --> 00:12:18.403
It's what you make it.

00:12:18.403 --> 00:12:19.361
Nana's version of that was,

00:12:19.361 --> 00:12:21.511
"Don't half-ass it."

00:12:21.511 --> 00:12:22.392
Right.

00:12:22.392 --> 00:12:25.441
Which we've talked about.

00:12:25.441 --> 00:12:27.885
I've had a hard time.

00:12:27.885 --> 00:12:30.135
There's not a lot of mottos

00:12:31.302 --> 00:12:35.501
that ring in my head that were
wants just pieces of advice.

00:12:35.501 --> 00:12:38.302
I remember when we were going on a trip

00:12:38.302 --> 00:12:40.052
to shoot some videos,

00:12:42.369 --> 00:12:43.937
a guy told me a guy me,

00:12:43.937 --> 00:12:45.390
a guy who I admire he said,

00:12:45.390 --> 00:12:46.466
"Hey just remember,

00:12:46.466 --> 00:12:50.409
it's better to have a good
story than a good time."

00:12:50.409 --> 00:12:52.613
And I kind of like that because,

00:12:52.613 --> 00:12:55.113
we're trying to make a...

00:12:55.113 --> 00:12:55.962
If you're in a...

00:12:55.962 --> 00:12:58.453
and I've talked about
this before on the show,

00:12:58.453 --> 00:13:01.002
if you're in a situation,

00:13:01.002 --> 00:13:02.252
and it's sucks,

00:13:03.894 --> 00:13:07.190
you're like, "well, at least
I can tell a story about it

00:13:07.190 --> 00:13:08.864
because I have a podcast."

00:13:08.864 --> 00:13:10.741
Or you have friends or
people who are listening.

00:13:10.741 --> 00:13:12.838
And yeah, I like telling a good story.

00:13:12.838 --> 00:13:15.436
So that's always helped
give me perspective

00:13:15.436 --> 00:13:17.390
when I'm going through something crappy.

00:13:17.390 --> 00:13:19.468
But it's a little bit weird to say it

00:13:19.468 --> 00:13:21.211
before you're in that situation.

00:13:21.211 --> 00:13:22.957
Well, especially because,

00:13:22.957 --> 00:13:27.038
I think that being present is actually--

00:13:27.038 --> 00:13:27.871
Yeah.

00:13:27.871 --> 00:13:29.371
A better advice.

00:13:30.558 --> 00:13:32.620
I understand, if you're going
through a difficult time,

00:13:32.620 --> 00:13:34.781
it's just better to have a
good story than a good time.

00:13:34.781 --> 00:13:35.623
If you're in the middle like,

00:13:35.623 --> 00:13:38.314
it's one of those crazy vacations

00:13:38.314 --> 00:13:39.147
where everything's going wrong.

00:13:39.147 --> 00:13:41.314
This sucks but at least,

00:13:42.806 --> 00:13:43.770
if I live to tell it,

00:13:43.770 --> 00:13:44.696
I will tell it.

00:13:44.696 --> 00:13:47.847
And usually what I say
in those situations is,

00:13:47.847 --> 00:13:50.430
"Man this makes a great story."

00:13:52.873 --> 00:13:55.681
I'm up shits creek without a paddle but,

00:13:55.681 --> 00:13:57.091
at least it's a good story.

00:13:57.091 --> 00:13:57.924
Like you said,

00:13:57.924 --> 00:14:01.892
especially when you have
a place to tell the story.

00:14:01.892 --> 00:14:03.245
But I don't think that,

00:14:03.245 --> 00:14:06.912
if I'm getting ready
to go do something fun,

00:14:08.966 --> 00:14:11.263
"better to have a good
story than a good time"

00:14:11.263 --> 00:14:12.214
is good advice,

00:14:12.214 --> 00:14:14.874
because what about the joy

00:14:14.874 --> 00:14:15.707
Yeah.

00:14:15.707 --> 00:14:17.872
That is available for me in the present?

00:14:17.872 --> 00:14:18.830
Yeah.

00:14:18.830 --> 00:14:21.071
I don't think it was great
advice in that sense.

00:14:21.071 --> 00:14:23.488
But I still really like it.

00:14:24.894 --> 00:14:26.144
What about,

00:14:26.144 --> 00:14:28.257
"Remember who you are."

00:14:28.257 --> 00:14:31.251
Parents send their kids
out into some something

00:14:31.251 --> 00:14:32.361
that they're gonna do

00:14:32.361 --> 00:14:34.195
and they come to the front door

00:14:34.195 --> 00:14:35.028
and they're like,

00:14:35.028 --> 00:14:38.223
"Hey, hey, hey, remember who you are."

00:14:38.223 --> 00:14:39.891
Have you ever said that
one of your children?

00:14:39.891 --> 00:14:41.883
Ah, sometimes I'd be like,

00:14:41.883 --> 00:14:43.378
" Remember you're a Neal."

00:14:45.564 --> 00:14:48.440
And what is that supposed to communicate?

00:14:48.440 --> 00:14:49.429
Don't embarrass me.

00:14:53.074 --> 00:14:54.991
I don't say that often.

00:14:57.943 --> 00:15:01.883
I don't find myself every giving these

00:15:01.883 --> 00:15:04.966
quotable pieces of advice to my kids.

00:15:07.595 --> 00:15:09.449
Yeah, not quotable,

00:15:09.449 --> 00:15:11.066
not bite size is more.

00:15:11.066 --> 00:15:12.409
But what I am guilty of,

00:15:12.409 --> 00:15:15.941
and I know you're just
as guilty of it as I am,

00:15:15.941 --> 00:15:19.845
is giving advice in general to our kids.

00:15:19.845 --> 00:15:22.178
It's actually something that

00:15:24.384 --> 00:15:28.716
Jessie and I have talked
a lot about is that,

00:15:28.716 --> 00:15:33.221
sometimes we get into a
conversation with our kids,

00:15:33.221 --> 00:15:35.122
they're just telling us about their day.

00:15:35.122 --> 00:15:36.596
They're telling us
about something they did

00:15:36.596 --> 00:15:38.852
or something they're going to do.

00:15:38.852 --> 00:15:42.578
And then there's like this
dad button that gets pressed

00:15:42.578 --> 00:15:43.653
and the dad button responds will like

00:15:43.653 --> 00:15:45.438
And you get this response, now,

00:15:45.438 --> 00:15:46.927
"Well, if you're gonna do that,

00:15:46.927 --> 00:15:48.432
think about this.

00:15:48.432 --> 00:15:50.857
Or when did that happen?

00:15:50.857 --> 00:15:52.786
What did you learn then, blah blah blah."

00:15:52.786 --> 00:15:54.084
It's like--

00:15:54.084 --> 00:15:54.917
Unsolicited.

00:15:54.917 --> 00:15:57.859
If you've got a friend
who talks like that,

00:15:57.859 --> 00:16:01.866
like every single time
they tell you something,

00:16:01.866 --> 00:16:03.360
you tell them something,

00:16:03.360 --> 00:16:05.299
they give you advice about it.

00:16:05.299 --> 00:16:06.132
That's not--

00:16:06.132 --> 00:16:07.051
What do you think I am, a screw up?

00:16:07.051 --> 00:16:09.723
I'm telling you this
so that we can connect,

00:16:09.723 --> 00:16:11.465
because I thought you
might find it interesting

00:16:11.465 --> 00:16:13.380
because you might be able
to add something to it,

00:16:13.380 --> 00:16:16.230
or you might be able to respond
to it with a perspective.

00:16:16.230 --> 00:16:17.063
Yeah.

00:16:17.063 --> 00:16:17.896
But not to then tell me

00:16:17.896 --> 00:16:19.336
what I should have done differently,

00:16:19.336 --> 00:16:21.076
or what I should have kept in mind,

00:16:21.076 --> 00:16:22.574
and I do that with my kids too often.

00:16:22.574 --> 00:16:26.710
I find when my kids talk
about their friends,

00:16:26.710 --> 00:16:30.292
things that are happening
with their friends,

00:16:30.292 --> 00:16:32.632
I am apparently filtering the conversation

00:16:32.632 --> 00:16:35.333
through an analysis of,

00:16:35.333 --> 00:16:37.726
"Do I approve of their friends?"

00:16:37.726 --> 00:16:38.559
'Cause--

00:16:38.559 --> 00:16:40.382
Oh, yeah.
Your friends is so powerful.

00:16:40.382 --> 00:16:41.786
There's probably a piece
of advice in there.

00:16:41.786 --> 00:16:45.126
"You are who you hang out with."

00:16:45.126 --> 00:16:47.221
It's probably a catchier version of that?

00:16:47.221 --> 00:16:51.711
But I think that I filter
things through that platitude,

00:16:51.711 --> 00:16:54.632
so then, when they're just
talking about their day

00:16:54.632 --> 00:16:56.369
or something that happened I'm like,

00:16:56.369 --> 00:17:00.747
"Well you know if you're
that type of person,

00:17:00.747 --> 00:17:02.969
if they do that then that's
not really a true friend.

00:17:02.969 --> 00:17:06.000
A true friend is someone who
blah blah blah blah blah."

00:17:06.000 --> 00:17:09.417
I end up analyzing and trying to protect.

00:17:11.076 --> 00:17:14.283
But I guess you wanna get to a
point where they're actually,

00:17:14.283 --> 00:17:15.894
it's solicited advice.

00:17:15.894 --> 00:17:17.241
My kids are asking,

00:17:17.241 --> 00:17:18.422
"Hey I'm in this situation

00:17:18.422 --> 00:17:21.056
or this person said this to me,

00:17:21.056 --> 00:17:22.613
I don't know how to respond or you know."

00:17:22.613 --> 00:17:25.284
Well, and if they don't do that,

00:17:25.284 --> 00:17:27.772
I think that there and I
don't have this perfected.

00:17:27.772 --> 00:17:31.310
I don't even have it
immediately understood,

00:17:31.310 --> 00:17:36.003
but the thing that I'm trying
to do now is ask questions,

00:17:36.003 --> 00:17:37.390
in a way that's not like a detective,

00:17:37.390 --> 00:17:39.145
not like a leading question.

00:17:39.145 --> 00:17:40.353
So I seem like I'm manipulating,

00:17:40.353 --> 00:17:42.381
but okay, I'm having this conversation.

00:17:42.381 --> 00:17:44.629
There is something that I feel like I need

00:17:44.629 --> 00:17:45.741
to communicate about this.

00:17:45.741 --> 00:17:47.600
Because I still do have advice,

00:17:47.600 --> 00:17:48.890
I have been around longer,

00:17:48.890 --> 00:17:50.272
I have a perspective on this,

00:17:50.272 --> 00:17:52.590
And I think I got a
little wisdom about this.

00:17:52.590 --> 00:17:55.022
And our kids left to their own devices,

00:17:55.022 --> 00:17:56.892
don't ask for advices.

00:17:56.892 --> 00:17:57.766
Right.

00:17:57.766 --> 00:18:00.433
And so then I'll ask a question.

00:18:02.326 --> 00:18:03.422
My kids are smart enough to know

00:18:03.422 --> 00:18:04.420
what I'm getting at,

00:18:04.420 --> 00:18:06.739
but at least it doesn't come across like,

00:18:06.739 --> 00:18:08.550
"Well, did you know,

00:18:08.550 --> 00:18:10.860
here is the thing that
I'm going to tell you."

00:18:10.860 --> 00:18:11.693
But it's more like,

00:18:11.693 --> 00:18:12.582
"Have you thought about this?

00:18:12.582 --> 00:18:14.992
Or how you feeling about it?"

00:18:14.992 --> 00:18:16.159
At least it gets them talking.

00:18:16.159 --> 00:18:18.519
And maybe you get someplace
where they come to a conclusion.

00:18:18.519 --> 00:18:19.352
I don't know,

00:18:19.352 --> 00:18:20.346
It's tough being a dad.

00:18:20.346 --> 00:18:22.783
But, we don't have to come
up with all the advice,

00:18:22.783 --> 00:18:24.565
we've got some great advice from people.

00:18:24.565 --> 00:18:26.412
We're gonna start going through it.

00:18:26.412 --> 00:18:27.245
But first,

00:18:28.538 --> 00:18:32.025
we're just going to gently advise you

00:18:32.025 --> 00:18:34.192
to check out mythical.com.

00:18:35.275 --> 00:18:36.261
We say it every week,

00:18:36.261 --> 00:18:39.168
but every week there's
a little something new.

00:18:39.168 --> 00:18:40.792
There's sales going on.

00:18:40.792 --> 00:18:42.709
There's stuff going on.

00:18:45.180 --> 00:18:46.561
You think we got hoodies,

00:18:46.561 --> 00:18:48.613
you think we got T-shirts?

00:18:48.613 --> 00:18:49.818
Yeah, we got that.

00:18:49.818 --> 00:18:54.160
But we got lots of other stuff
like pins and stickers and--

00:18:54.160 --> 00:18:55.297
Well, and we've got

00:18:55.297 --> 00:18:59.892
the whole Feel Good
Mythical Morning ensemble,

00:18:59.892 --> 00:19:01.103
that we came up with.

00:19:01.103 --> 00:19:03.544
We had the Feel Good
Mythical Morning Hoodie,

00:19:03.544 --> 00:19:06.504
but now they got the Feel
Good Mythical Morning T-shirt.

00:19:06.504 --> 00:19:08.353
We got the Feel Good
Mythical Morning sticker

00:19:08.353 --> 00:19:09.917
and enamel pin.

00:19:09.917 --> 00:19:12.669
So if you wanna feel good.

00:19:12.669 --> 00:19:13.650
That's our advice.

00:19:13.650 --> 00:19:15.255
"Feel good."

00:19:15.255 --> 00:19:16.299
If it feels good, do it.

00:19:16.299 --> 00:19:18.984
And if you need some help with that,

00:19:18.984 --> 00:19:21.175
just go to Mythical.com.

00:19:21.175 --> 00:19:22.563
Okay, let's get started.

00:19:22.563 --> 00:19:24.813
Sometimes I tell my kids,

00:19:25.671 --> 00:19:27.601
"Don't think just do."

00:19:27.601 --> 00:19:28.768
It's probably.

00:19:29.785 --> 00:19:30.618
And ironic--

00:19:30.618 --> 00:19:31.762
In what context?

00:19:31.762 --> 00:19:34.395
Usually when they're standing
on the edge of a pool,

00:19:34.395 --> 00:19:36.607
and they're afraid to
jump in because it's cold.

00:19:36.607 --> 00:19:38.946
I was gonna say in like
an athletic context?

00:19:38.946 --> 00:19:39.779
Yeah.

00:19:39.779 --> 00:19:41.512
That's great advice.

00:19:41.512 --> 00:19:43.099
Because the thinking and
the doing is supposed

00:19:43.099 --> 00:19:44.536
to be happening in sync.

00:19:44.536 --> 00:19:48.579
If you're thinking, the guy's
already gone around you.

00:19:48.579 --> 00:19:50.570
Somebody else has jumped in the pool.

00:19:50.570 --> 00:19:52.692
It's also ironic because
Before you.

00:19:52.692 --> 00:19:56.101
It's not what I want them to
do in their non athletic lives.

00:19:56.101 --> 00:19:58.654
Which is the vast majority of their life.

00:19:58.654 --> 00:19:59.968
I'm not cutting on my kids.

00:19:59.968 --> 00:20:00.801
Yeah, it did.

00:20:00.801 --> 00:20:02.509
Your kids are non athletic?

00:20:02.509 --> 00:20:04.337
My kids are not sports minded.

00:20:04.337 --> 00:20:05.938
Yeah, but I wouldn't say
they're non athletic,

00:20:05.938 --> 00:20:07.990
they just might not be
like super into sports.

00:20:07.990 --> 00:20:11.880
But if you threw like a hot dog at them,

00:20:11.880 --> 00:20:14.081
they probably catch it.

00:20:14.081 --> 00:20:15.042
Don't you think?

00:20:15.042 --> 00:20:16.180
I'll test that.
Try that

00:20:16.180 --> 00:20:17.187
I'll test that today.

00:20:17.187 --> 00:20:19.109
That's the ultimate test of athleticism.

00:20:19.109 --> 00:20:21.288
Throw a hot dog straight from the fridge

00:20:21.288 --> 00:20:23.026
right at your child's face.

00:20:23.026 --> 00:20:25.053
And if it hits him in the face,

00:20:25.053 --> 00:20:26.719
hopefully they're smart.

00:20:26.719 --> 00:20:29.653
Because they're not athletic.
They're not athletic.

00:20:29.653 --> 00:20:30.646
Okay.

00:20:30.646 --> 00:20:31.479
Give me some advice.

00:20:31.479 --> 00:20:34.312
Ariana bearscivilles on Twitter.

00:20:37.845 --> 00:20:38.678
My grandmother--

00:20:38.678 --> 00:20:40.788
It's bearskvlls but the U is a V.

00:20:40.788 --> 00:20:42.573
Yeah, I was just trying to be phonetic.

00:20:42.573 --> 00:20:45.002
My grandmother always used to tell me,

00:20:45.002 --> 00:20:47.601
"look towards the sunshine,

00:20:47.601 --> 00:20:50.189
and the shadows will fall behind you."

00:20:50.189 --> 00:20:54.049
That's true from like
a physics standpoint.

00:20:54.049 --> 00:20:55.123
Every time the sun is out,

00:20:55.123 --> 00:20:57.008
it makes me feel like
she's watching over me

00:20:57.008 --> 00:21:00.264
and making sure I'm taking care of myself.

00:21:00.264 --> 00:21:02.556
Isn't it so sweet

00:21:02.556 --> 00:21:05.237
if you have these memories of grandparents

00:21:05.237 --> 00:21:06.779
or someone that means something to you,

00:21:06.779 --> 00:21:09.518
that even if they passed,

00:21:09.518 --> 00:21:14.518
that they passed along something
that can echo like them?

00:21:15.009 --> 00:21:16.377
For her to be so positive.

00:21:16.377 --> 00:21:17.706
Look towards the sunshine.

00:21:17.706 --> 00:21:19.996
Actually, be positive

00:21:19.996 --> 00:21:23.079
and the shadows will fall behind you.

00:21:24.586 --> 00:21:28.419
I consider myself a
generally positive person,

00:21:31.099 --> 00:21:33.498
but i think is more of a
coping mechanism with me.

00:21:33.498 --> 00:21:35.054
It's like, oh gosh.

00:21:35.054 --> 00:21:36.688
If I can convince myself that

00:21:36.688 --> 00:21:38.606
when things are not going well,

00:21:38.606 --> 00:21:40.606
'cause that does happen,

00:21:41.969 --> 00:21:45.059
I'll feel better if I can minimize.

00:21:45.059 --> 00:21:47.022
But I've met some people that

00:21:47.022 --> 00:21:49.541
seem more like genuinely positive people.

00:21:49.541 --> 00:21:51.541
They have a gift for it.

00:21:54.198 --> 00:21:55.741
They can turn from the shadow

00:21:55.741 --> 00:21:58.338
and see the sunshine in a moment.

00:21:58.338 --> 00:21:59.847
Maybe they're just so good at applying

00:21:59.847 --> 00:22:01.515
this kind of advice that

00:22:01.515 --> 00:22:04.598
they're secretly catastrophizing but.

00:22:06.606 --> 00:22:07.959
The advice is for people

00:22:07.959 --> 00:22:09.799
who don't have a natural gift at it,

00:22:09.799 --> 00:22:11.880
because they don't need the advice.

00:22:11.880 --> 00:22:13.797
So it does involve you.

00:22:15.731 --> 00:22:18.812
You can make a choice in any situation

00:22:18.812 --> 00:22:21.244
what you're going to focus on.

00:22:21.244 --> 00:22:22.077
Mm-hmm.

00:22:22.077 --> 00:22:23.335
And how you're gonna see it,

00:22:23.335 --> 00:22:25.178
how you're gonna process it.

00:22:25.178 --> 00:22:26.742
I mean, this advice
has been given in a lot

00:22:26.742 --> 00:22:27.867
of different contexts.

00:22:27.867 --> 00:22:29.536
You've got keep on the sunny side of life,

00:22:29.536 --> 00:22:30.869
that great song.

00:22:31.986 --> 00:22:34.066
But then Locke and I actually had

00:22:34.066 --> 00:22:36.885
this conversation the other day,

00:22:36.885 --> 00:22:38.169
and talking about...

00:22:38.169 --> 00:22:40.441
And I'm sure that you've been
having similar conversations

00:22:40.441 --> 00:22:43.858
with your teenage kids and all your kids.

00:22:44.799 --> 00:22:49.799
But I think that COVID has
been especially frustrating

00:22:49.911 --> 00:22:51.744
for teenagers because,

00:22:53.391 --> 00:22:55.010
you just think about
what your life was like

00:22:55.010 --> 00:22:57.011
when you were 16,

00:22:57.011 --> 00:23:00.594
and to have all these
restrictions put on--

00:23:02.337 --> 00:23:03.524
You have so much life to live.

00:23:03.524 --> 00:23:05.588
Where you can go, yeah.

00:23:05.588 --> 00:23:06.498
You haven't lived most of it.

00:23:06.498 --> 00:23:07.331
Right, and you're like oh--

00:23:07.331 --> 00:23:08.225
It's frustrating.

00:23:08.225 --> 00:23:11.392
The summer of my 16th year is, what?

00:23:12.515 --> 00:23:15.562
I gotta wear masks if I
wanna be around my friends

00:23:15.562 --> 00:23:17.846
and they thought and
probably be outside with them

00:23:17.846 --> 00:23:19.954
and I to ask them questions about,

00:23:19.954 --> 00:23:22.608
where they've been and where
their families have been.

00:23:22.608 --> 00:23:24.284
This is incredibly frustrating,

00:23:24.284 --> 00:23:27.723
especially considering the fact that,

00:23:27.723 --> 00:23:30.516
if a teenager gets the Coronavirus,

00:23:30.516 --> 00:23:34.433
that mean the chances of
even them even knowing

00:23:35.340 --> 00:23:37.482
they've got it, is pretty small, right?

00:23:37.482 --> 00:23:39.180
Is this not going to
affect them personally,

00:23:39.180 --> 00:23:41.527
so it's almost completely,

00:23:41.527 --> 00:23:44.705
selfless thing that they have to do.

00:23:44.705 --> 00:23:48.569
And so, we've had a lot
of conversations about

00:23:48.569 --> 00:23:50.773
how difficult this has been.

00:23:50.773 --> 00:23:54.766
In the in the way that I gave
him some advice the other day

00:23:54.766 --> 00:23:55.939
'cause I was like--

00:23:55.939 --> 00:23:57.315
Look towards the sunshine

00:23:57.315 --> 00:23:58.148
and the shadows will fall behind you.

00:23:58.148 --> 00:24:00.063
Well, that's what I should have said.

00:24:00.063 --> 00:24:02.867
But what I said is like,

00:24:02.867 --> 00:24:04.924
and this is not as good of advice as this,

00:24:04.924 --> 00:24:06.126
'cause it's not as simple.

00:24:06.126 --> 00:24:08.201
But I was like,

00:24:08.201 --> 00:24:09.877
"You're going through something right now.

00:24:09.877 --> 00:24:12.467
And I can write a story about it,

00:24:12.467 --> 00:24:15.238
and I can write two
different scripts, right?

00:24:15.238 --> 00:24:19.155
And one script is that
this is really difficult

00:24:20.227 --> 00:24:21.519
and really restrictive,

00:24:21.519 --> 00:24:25.339
and your parents are
taking this too seriously,

00:24:25.339 --> 00:24:28.992
and all the negative
things that you can see."

00:24:28.992 --> 00:24:29.825
Mm-hmm.
But then there's

00:24:29.825 --> 00:24:31.060
the positive side of the script,

00:24:31.060 --> 00:24:32.653
which is when you look back on this,

00:24:32.653 --> 00:24:33.486
you'd be like,

00:24:33.486 --> 00:24:35.548
I lived through this really weird time

00:24:35.548 --> 00:24:37.916
and it made me reevaluate my friendships.

00:24:37.916 --> 00:24:41.385
And we kind of went through
like all the positive way

00:24:41.385 --> 00:24:43.216
that the things that you could focus on.

00:24:43.216 --> 00:24:46.342
It's like your parents are
healthy, you're healthy,

00:24:46.342 --> 00:24:48.160
nobody that we know has gotten this.

00:24:48.160 --> 00:24:52.485
There's not other health
conditions that we're already...

00:24:52.485 --> 00:24:55.317
No one in our family is immunocompromised.

00:24:55.317 --> 00:24:57.583
You start sort of counting
all your blessings

00:24:57.583 --> 00:25:01.594
and the things that you
have been privileged to,

00:25:01.594 --> 00:25:02.892
the position that you're in.

00:25:02.892 --> 00:25:04.539
And you're like, oh, okay, that helps.

00:25:04.539 --> 00:25:07.064
You turn towards the positive.

00:25:07.064 --> 00:25:08.118
And then I was like,

00:25:08.118 --> 00:25:11.767
because your circumstances
are not gonna change, right?

00:25:11.767 --> 00:25:14.232
We're gonna be in this for a while,

00:25:14.232 --> 00:25:16.661
and if you're expecting me

00:25:16.661 --> 00:25:18.649
to do something to make it okay for you,

00:25:18.649 --> 00:25:21.392
or you're expecting your
friends to do something.

00:25:21.392 --> 00:25:22.888
No, the only thing that you can do

00:25:22.888 --> 00:25:24.393
is you can write your own script,

00:25:24.393 --> 00:25:26.070
you can turn towards the sunshine,

00:25:26.070 --> 00:25:28.902
turn away from the darkness.

00:25:28.902 --> 00:25:31.008
And I'm kind of giving myself that advice

00:25:31.008 --> 00:25:34.063
as I give it to him because,

00:25:34.063 --> 00:25:36.501
this has been difficult
for everybody, right?

00:25:36.501 --> 00:25:37.378
But to say like,

00:25:37.378 --> 00:25:39.581
all right, where can I be grateful?

00:25:39.581 --> 00:25:41.248
What can I focus on?

00:25:42.338 --> 00:25:44.414
Because I have a whole
lot to be thankful for.

00:25:44.414 --> 00:25:45.247
And when I do that,

00:25:45.247 --> 00:25:48.683
all of a sudden it's like, okay, yeah,

00:25:48.683 --> 00:25:50.206
it's truly helpful.

00:25:50.206 --> 00:25:54.456
The Black Sheep responded
to our prompt and said,

00:25:55.861 --> 00:25:59.376
"Life changing advice was
to always remember Sonder

00:25:59.376 --> 00:26:02.385
S-O-N-D-E-R,

00:26:02.385 --> 00:26:06.323
before getting upset at somebody
else over petty things."

00:26:06.323 --> 00:26:09.823
And then she put the definition of sonder,

00:26:10.744 --> 00:26:11.992
which I had never heard that definition.

00:26:11.992 --> 00:26:15.763
Oh, I actually had this pulled up.

00:26:15.763 --> 00:26:16.596
Not.

00:26:17.502 --> 00:26:18.335
Sonder,

00:26:20.214 --> 00:26:21.794
it is a company.

00:26:21.794 --> 00:26:22.627
No.

00:26:22.627 --> 00:26:24.615
That is like Airbnb,

00:26:24.615 --> 00:26:28.122
but people never live in the homes,

00:26:28.122 --> 00:26:32.289
but it's like a curated
hotel in a home experience

00:26:34.300 --> 00:26:35.371
that no one ever lived in.

00:26:35.371 --> 00:26:36.456
That ain't what we're talking.

00:26:36.456 --> 00:26:40.254
Right, 'cause it was as
a term coined in 2012

00:26:40.254 --> 00:26:42.337
by John Koring or Koenig,

00:26:44.755 --> 00:26:47.504
whose project "The Dictionary
of Obscure Sorrows"

00:26:47.504 --> 00:26:49.562
aims to come up with
new words for emotions

00:26:49.562 --> 00:26:50.929
that currently lack words.

00:26:50.929 --> 00:26:51.921
Mm-hmm.

00:26:51.921 --> 00:26:55.157
And this one is inspired by German sonder,

00:26:55.157 --> 00:26:58.902
which means special and French sonder

00:26:58.902 --> 00:27:00.884
which means to probe.

00:27:00.884 --> 00:27:02.696
And his definition is,

00:27:02.696 --> 00:27:05.183
the profound feeling of
realizing that everyone,

00:27:05.183 --> 00:27:07.662
including strangers passed in the street,

00:27:07.662 --> 00:27:10.191
has a life as complex as one's own,

00:27:10.191 --> 00:27:11.644
which they are constantly living,

00:27:11.644 --> 00:27:15.753
despite one's personal
lack of awareness of it.

00:27:15.753 --> 00:27:17.553
Go back to the definition she posted.

00:27:17.553 --> 00:27:19.144
It's a little more succinct.

00:27:19.144 --> 00:27:20.544
Sonder noun.

00:27:20.544 --> 00:27:24.548
The realization that each
random passerby is living a life

00:27:24.548 --> 00:27:27.298
as vivid and complex as your own.

00:27:29.973 --> 00:27:30.806
I love this.

00:27:30.806 --> 00:27:31.889
I wanna remember this word.

00:27:31.889 --> 00:27:33.158
Imma say it three times.

00:27:33.158 --> 00:27:35.075
Sonder, sonder, sonder.

00:27:35.979 --> 00:27:37.767
I've got it.

00:27:37.767 --> 00:27:39.667
I'm doubtful but.

00:27:39.667 --> 00:27:41.334
Behind every face,

00:27:42.979 --> 00:27:43.873
there's a story.

00:27:43.873 --> 00:27:45.310
Look over sonder.

00:27:45.310 --> 00:27:47.730
And that is something
that we've talked about.

00:27:47.730 --> 00:27:50.165
When you interact with people,

00:27:50.165 --> 00:27:52.613
you never know what's
going on in their life.

00:27:52.613 --> 00:27:54.339
I tend to immediately think about

00:27:54.339 --> 00:27:56.347
seeing strangers get angry like a road--

00:27:56.347 --> 00:27:57.180
On the internet.

00:27:57.180 --> 00:27:59.234
Like a road rage situation.

00:27:59.234 --> 00:28:01.016
Okay, well, we can talk
about the internet too.

00:28:01.016 --> 00:28:03.099
But when you see somebody

00:28:04.203 --> 00:28:07.543
or you read their comment or whatever,

00:28:07.543 --> 00:28:11.710
it's tough not to just
react to how it impacts you

00:28:13.142 --> 00:28:14.435
or how it strikes you.

00:28:14.435 --> 00:28:15.915
Even if it's just a passerby

00:28:15.915 --> 00:28:18.302
that it is not impacting you at all.

00:28:18.302 --> 00:28:20.969
But it's a good exercise to say,

00:28:22.825 --> 00:28:26.568
and there's a story being lived out there.

00:28:26.568 --> 00:28:28.594
That person is going through something.

00:28:28.594 --> 00:28:29.624
And when somebody does something

00:28:29.624 --> 00:28:32.874
that's like a head scratcher, or worse,

00:28:34.467 --> 00:28:37.669
that person is lashing out in anger.

00:28:37.669 --> 00:28:39.822
Just stop and sonderise it,

00:28:39.822 --> 00:28:41.870
say you know what,

00:28:41.870 --> 00:28:43.555
I've been really angry before

00:28:43.555 --> 00:28:45.217
and sometimes it's not justified

00:28:45.217 --> 00:28:46.920
but there were other
things going on in my life

00:28:46.920 --> 00:28:47.837
that I knew about

00:28:47.837 --> 00:28:50.556
or couldn't put my finger on,

00:28:50.556 --> 00:28:54.056
just to have a little pause for compassion

00:28:55.260 --> 00:28:57.994
And also when you are about
to get angry at someone

00:28:57.994 --> 00:28:58.827
for something.

00:28:58.827 --> 00:28:59.660
Mm-hmm.

00:28:59.660 --> 00:29:00.493
Right, when they say

00:29:00.493 --> 00:29:03.056
or do something meant
unintentionally, intentionally

00:29:03.056 --> 00:29:04.139
or otherwise.

00:29:05.082 --> 00:29:08.929
When you think oh, it's
just empathy, right?

00:29:08.929 --> 00:29:10.475
It's putting yourself
in somebody else's shoes

00:29:10.475 --> 00:29:12.252
and just understanding that yeah.

00:29:12.252 --> 00:29:14.835
And they also are worried about

00:29:15.672 --> 00:29:18.723
the way that they look and
they sound to the same degree

00:29:18.723 --> 00:29:19.724
that you are right?

00:29:19.724 --> 00:29:22.203
Everyone's a bit obsessed with themselves.

00:29:22.203 --> 00:29:25.492
I know that I'm gonna
make mistakes in life,

00:29:25.492 --> 00:29:27.409
I'm gonna wrong people.

00:29:29.559 --> 00:29:31.560
I'm gonna place my needs
above other people.

00:29:31.560 --> 00:29:33.400
I'm gonna do things that I regret

00:29:33.400 --> 00:29:35.317
and then I'm sorry for.

00:29:36.710 --> 00:29:40.362
And I'm gonna do things
that are also misinterpreted

00:29:40.362 --> 00:29:42.332
and can be misinterpreted a lot worse

00:29:42.332 --> 00:29:43.588
than they actually were.

00:29:43.588 --> 00:29:44.421
Mm-hmm.

00:29:45.829 --> 00:29:49.803
And I want people to give
me the benefit of the doubt

00:29:49.803 --> 00:29:54.398
when entering into a situation
and a dialogue with me about

00:29:54.398 --> 00:29:58.172
whatever mistake I made
or potentially made.

00:29:58.172 --> 00:30:01.312
It goes a long way for
people to believe the bests.

00:30:01.312 --> 00:30:03.901
I don't know if you meant
this when you said it,

00:30:03.901 --> 00:30:06.237
but it struck me in this way.

00:30:06.237 --> 00:30:07.070
Mm-hmm.

00:30:07.070 --> 00:30:08.545
Or it seems like you said this,

00:30:08.545 --> 00:30:11.161
I don't know if that's what you intended.

00:30:11.161 --> 00:30:14.066
Is an example of opening a conversation.

00:30:14.066 --> 00:30:18.132
It's not like, that is the worst thing.

00:30:18.132 --> 00:30:20.147
That's stupid or that's ignorant,

00:30:20.147 --> 00:30:24.597
or it could just be a mistake
or a misunderstanding.

00:30:24.597 --> 00:30:27.781
Now believing the best in some situations,

00:30:27.781 --> 00:30:30.327
I think the best that you can
believe is still pretty bad

00:30:30.327 --> 00:30:31.403
in a certain situation.

00:30:31.403 --> 00:30:32.236
Of course.

00:30:32.236 --> 00:30:33.751
There are people who do bad things

00:30:33.751 --> 00:30:34.905
Like I said, people should
be held accountably.

00:30:34.905 --> 00:30:36.375
And they do it on purpose.

00:30:36.375 --> 00:30:37.585
This is not about excusing anyone

00:30:37.585 --> 00:30:39.752
No.
For their behavior.

00:30:42.107 --> 00:30:44.251
In general believing the best in people

00:30:44.251 --> 00:30:45.580
and applying that filter,

00:30:45.580 --> 00:30:49.080
whenever I find myself getting up in arms.

00:30:52.035 --> 00:30:56.506
That saying implies that
you want to attack, right?

00:30:56.506 --> 00:30:58.754
And I think we get to that
with another piece of advice,

00:30:58.754 --> 00:30:59.787
so maybe I'll leave it at that.

00:30:59.787 --> 00:31:02.187
But I do wanna just remember sonder

00:31:02.187 --> 00:31:04.395
and I think it's a good...

00:31:04.395 --> 00:31:07.448
For some reason, because
I don't live in New York

00:31:07.448 --> 00:31:09.978
and we visit New York City.

00:31:09.978 --> 00:31:11.409
I always think of that environment

00:31:11.409 --> 00:31:13.181
'cause you're walking around

00:31:13.181 --> 00:31:16.377
and you're seeing so many faces.

00:31:16.377 --> 00:31:18.354
That's what I picture with sonder.

00:31:18.354 --> 00:31:20.155
Is that like I'm on a street corner,

00:31:20.155 --> 00:31:23.246
I'm always overwhelmed when
I go back in a good way.

00:31:23.246 --> 00:31:26.247
"Well look at all these people just moving

00:31:26.247 --> 00:31:28.330
with the traffic lights."

00:31:31.318 --> 00:31:34.735
Behind every set of eyes there's a story.

00:31:38.623 --> 00:31:39.456
There's something in that

00:31:39.456 --> 00:31:42.289
that kinda makes me swell up with,

00:31:43.191 --> 00:31:45.358
like pride for be a human.

00:31:46.264 --> 00:31:49.373
There's a vivid experience there

00:31:49.373 --> 00:31:51.132
that I know nothing about.

00:31:51.132 --> 00:31:51.978
Yeah, I think about that

00:31:51.978 --> 00:31:55.230
when I'm on elevators
with people sometimes.

00:31:55.230 --> 00:31:56.168
It's a fun practice.

00:31:56.168 --> 00:31:58.360
I think it's a good habit to cultivate.

00:31:58.360 --> 00:32:00.295
And then I don't talk to them,

00:32:00.295 --> 00:32:01.199
I don't say anything.

00:32:01.199 --> 00:32:03.160
Yeah,

00:32:03.160 --> 00:32:05.553
it could be problematic
being the elevator talker,

00:32:05.553 --> 00:32:07.249
but it is a good habit.

00:32:07.249 --> 00:32:09.665
I think it puts you in your place.

00:32:09.665 --> 00:32:10.820
When you see somebody say,

00:32:10.820 --> 00:32:13.041
"I wonder what their story is."

00:32:13.041 --> 00:32:15.037
And then it might be fun
just to make something up,

00:32:15.037 --> 00:32:16.977
just in your own mind,

00:32:16.977 --> 00:32:18.935
but as long as you don't convince yourself

00:32:18.935 --> 00:32:20.471
that's them and then say,

00:32:20.471 --> 00:32:24.202
"I can't believe that
you left the gate open

00:32:24.202 --> 00:32:26.682
on the tiger cage at the zoo."

00:32:26.682 --> 00:32:28.015
That's one of my favorite things about

00:32:28.015 --> 00:32:29.105
that Duplass brothers book.

00:32:29.105 --> 00:32:29.938
Oh, yeah.

00:32:29.938 --> 00:32:32.350
Is how they will sit in an
