00:45:40.533 --> 00:45:41.700
#EarBiscuits."

00:45:42.929 --> 00:45:44.285
Enter every conversation,

00:45:44.285 --> 00:45:48.202
assuming you have something
to learn, humility,

00:45:50.005 --> 00:45:51.457
In light of what's going on,

00:45:51.457 --> 00:45:53.457
and I've really tried to

00:45:57.805 --> 00:46:00.834
with the Black Lives Matter movement,

00:46:00.834 --> 00:46:03.001
right now just really say,

00:46:04.516 --> 00:46:05.787
" I'm gonna listen,

00:46:05.787 --> 00:46:06.949
I'm gonna learn,

00:46:06.949 --> 00:46:09.616
and I'm gonna make applications.

00:46:10.875 --> 00:46:13.439
It's really top of mind
for me in that way,

00:46:13.439 --> 00:46:16.519
but it's so important in general.

00:46:16.519 --> 00:46:18.936
I think it's not instinctive,

00:46:20.254 --> 00:46:23.587
it's instinctive to just react and okay.

00:46:24.757 --> 00:46:27.067
If someone's saying something
that could impact me

00:46:27.067 --> 00:46:32.067
or reflect on me or is someone
making an assertion about me,

00:46:33.910 --> 00:46:36.985
and then you instinctively take that in

00:46:36.985 --> 00:46:39.568
and then can react defensively.

00:46:40.941 --> 00:46:44.547
It's hard to walk a mile
in someone else's shoes,

00:46:44.547 --> 00:46:49.159
it takes practice to have
that kind of mindset.

00:46:49.159 --> 00:46:50.869
To step out of yourself

00:46:50.869 --> 00:46:52.452
and whatever it is,

00:46:53.768 --> 00:46:56.851
whatever issue impacts me and saying,

00:46:58.309 --> 00:47:00.947
"Okay, what is this person really saying?

00:47:00.947 --> 00:47:04.197
What can I learn in this conversation?"

00:47:05.154 --> 00:47:09.518
I think we've talked about how
we go in the party settings,

00:47:09.518 --> 00:47:11.216
and we're interacting with people

00:47:11.216 --> 00:47:12.864
and you're having these
little conversations,

00:47:12.864 --> 00:47:15.279
and the ones that are the
most stimulating to us

00:47:15.279 --> 00:47:16.693
are the ones that's like,

00:47:16.693 --> 00:47:18.940
when we have a mindset of,

00:47:18.940 --> 00:47:22.094
when somebody tells me what they do,

00:47:22.094 --> 00:47:23.888
if you pump a septic tank,

00:47:23.888 --> 00:47:27.212
or you're writing blockbuster films,

00:47:27.212 --> 00:47:30.339
we should be able to have
an engaging conversation

00:47:30.339 --> 00:47:31.172
with each person.

00:47:31.172 --> 00:47:33.255
And I think it's because,

00:47:34.124 --> 00:47:36.709
it's a little bit of this mentality of,

00:47:36.709 --> 00:47:38.319
there's always a question to ask,

00:47:38.319 --> 00:47:40.479
there's always something to be learned.

00:47:40.479 --> 00:47:41.312
Mm-hmm.

00:47:41.312 --> 00:47:43.542
It's like anybody can say,

00:47:43.542 --> 00:47:44.792
"I'm so and so,

00:47:45.903 --> 00:47:47.820
or I do this on Reddit,

00:47:48.942 --> 00:47:50.402
ask me anything."

00:47:50.402 --> 00:47:53.646
And underneath you will
find rewarding conversations

00:47:53.646 --> 00:47:55.802
when people have that mindset.

00:47:55.802 --> 00:47:57.395
It's rewarding for everybody.

00:47:57.395 --> 00:47:58.964
People feel valued,

00:47:58.964 --> 00:47:59.801
they'll feel they feel heard,

00:47:59.801 --> 00:48:01.562
and you you can learn something.

00:48:01.562 --> 00:48:04.032
You can learn something
from a total asshole.

00:48:04.032 --> 00:48:08.449
Mm-hmm, well I do think
that this is so applicable.

00:48:10.235 --> 00:48:13.005
we're just in the latest sort of

00:48:13.005 --> 00:48:15.433
polarizing national issue, right?

00:48:15.433 --> 00:48:16.830
Mm-hmm.

00:48:16.830 --> 00:48:21.128
This has been going on
for a long time now.

00:48:21.128 --> 00:48:25.100
The dream of the internet
connecting people resulted in

00:48:25.100 --> 00:48:26.670
the internet dividing people

00:48:26.670 --> 00:48:30.620
because now there's this
democratization of information,

00:48:30.620 --> 00:48:34.750
and people have separated into
these information bubbles.

00:48:34.750 --> 00:48:39.220
And you only talk with
people who agree with you,

00:48:39.220 --> 00:48:41.862
and then when you do
get into these occasions

00:48:41.862 --> 00:48:43.210
where you have a conversation with someone

00:48:43.210 --> 00:48:45.340
that you disagree with,

00:48:45.340 --> 00:48:47.620
the goal is not to find the truth

00:48:47.620 --> 00:48:49.141
between your two perspectives,

00:48:49.141 --> 00:48:52.035
the goal is to entrench yourself

00:48:52.035 --> 00:48:54.306
and defend your position at all costs,

00:48:54.306 --> 00:48:56.056
and I'm guilty of it.

00:48:58.523 --> 00:49:02.356
Like you said, it's just
kind of human nature.

00:49:03.285 --> 00:49:05.403
I'm thinking about,

00:49:05.403 --> 00:49:06.776
I don't know exactly how this relates,

00:49:06.776 --> 00:49:11.447
but this made me think of
this quote from Fight Club.

00:49:11.447 --> 00:49:14.753
Because I just watched Fight
Club recently with Locke.

00:49:14.753 --> 00:49:16.892
Rule number one.

00:49:16.892 --> 00:49:17.995
