00:55:39.847 --> 00:55:40.835
it could be a business,

00:55:40.835 --> 00:55:42.677
it could be a loved one,

00:55:42.677 --> 00:55:45.837
It could be a complete stranger.

00:55:45.837 --> 00:55:48.995
Someone where he needed
to accomplish something.

00:55:48.995 --> 00:55:49.828
In some way,

00:55:49.828 --> 00:55:51.668
he was gonna make a phone call to do that.

00:55:51.668 --> 00:55:52.501
Right.

00:55:52.501 --> 00:55:53.527
And then right there in front of him

00:55:53.527 --> 00:55:56.418
was his reminder to himself.

00:55:56.418 --> 00:55:58.830
"Invest in someone else's happiness."

00:55:58.830 --> 00:56:01.184
And here's an opportunity right now.

00:56:01.184 --> 00:56:03.890
It can be something as simple as,

00:56:03.890 --> 00:56:06.596
"I'm calling you for this reason A,

00:56:06.596 --> 00:56:08.644
but by the way B,

00:56:08.644 --> 00:56:10.646
I can say a little something
to make you happy."

00:56:10.646 --> 00:56:11.714
I can crack a joke,

00:56:11.714 --> 00:56:13.225
I can be smiling on the phone

00:56:13.225 --> 00:56:14.058
and now you can feel it.

00:56:14.058 --> 00:56:15.031
It might just change the tone

00:56:15.031 --> 00:56:15.864
of the way that you communicate.

00:56:15.864 --> 00:56:17.301
Yeah.

00:56:17.301 --> 00:56:20.643
The thing that it makes me think about is,

00:56:20.643 --> 00:56:22.663
I feel like I know nothing

00:56:22.663 --> 00:56:25.387
about Beth Ann's grandfather, right?

00:56:25.387 --> 00:56:26.415
I've never met the man.

00:56:26.415 --> 00:56:29.282
All I know is that he put
this note into a phonebook.

00:56:29.282 --> 00:56:30.115
Yeah.

00:56:30.115 --> 00:56:31.763
But I know a lot about him
because of that, right?

00:56:31.763 --> 00:56:32.596
Yeah.

00:56:32.596 --> 00:56:34.506
I know that whether he was
like this his entire life,

00:56:34.506 --> 00:56:37.369
or he came to the
conclusion later in life,

00:56:37.369 --> 00:56:41.640
that this is actually where his happiness,

00:56:41.640 --> 00:56:44.185
his true happiness lied, right?

00:56:44.185 --> 00:56:46.675
Is in investing in
somebody else's happiness.

00:56:46.675 --> 00:56:51.675
And man, it's such good advice
because everybody needs it,

00:56:52.152 --> 00:56:57.152
and we're all very naturally
resistant to understanding that

00:56:57.153 --> 00:56:59.495
because your natural impulse is to invest

00:56:59.495 --> 00:57:01.912
in your own happiness, right?

00:57:02.844 --> 00:57:05.101
It's to look out for yourself.

00:57:05.101 --> 00:57:07.069
It's to think about your circumstances.

00:57:07.069 --> 00:57:11.270
But we talked about the whole
the idea of the first mountain

00:57:11.270 --> 00:57:12.849
and the second mountain.

00:57:12.849 --> 00:57:14.133
The first mountain in life being about

00:57:14.133 --> 00:57:16.580
you getting to the top of
whatever your goals are.

00:57:16.580 --> 00:57:17.413
Mm-hmm.

00:57:17.413 --> 00:57:18.612
And then either you
fall off that mountain,

00:57:18.612 --> 00:57:19.445
or you get to the top.

00:57:19.445 --> 00:57:21.329
This is that David Brooks book,

00:57:21.329 --> 00:57:22.162
and then you're like,

00:57:22.162 --> 00:57:23.769
"Oh, there's another mountain
I should have been on."

00:57:23.769 --> 00:57:27.251
And that's the mountain of
investing in other people.

00:57:27.251 --> 00:57:30.578
And if you can get there at some point,

00:57:30.578 --> 00:57:33.498
and make your life where it
shifts from your interest.

00:57:33.498 --> 00:57:34.837
That can be very
Sounds like it happened

00:57:34.837 --> 00:57:35.920
with her grandfather.

00:57:35.920 --> 00:57:36.753
Rewarding.

00:57:36.753 --> 00:57:38.003
And the phrase,

00:57:39.400 --> 00:57:41.220
"pay it forward" comes to mind.

00:57:41.220 --> 00:57:44.380
The thing that I don't love about that is

00:57:44.380 --> 00:57:48.297
it implies that you've
received something good,

00:57:49.170 --> 00:57:52.469
and now it's a reminder to give it.

00:57:52.469 --> 00:57:53.976
I shouldn't pick it apart.

00:57:53.976 --> 00:57:55.990
I'm sure it's a great phrase,

00:57:55.990 --> 00:57:57.671
and is a good mantra,

00:57:57.671 --> 00:58:00.588
but the thing I like about this is,

00:58:01.505 --> 00:58:03.786
It's not contingent on your experience

00:58:03.786 --> 00:58:06.117
to invest in someone else's happiness.

00:58:06.117 --> 00:58:10.867
But when you do benefit from
someone enriching your life,

00:58:13.286 --> 00:58:16.453
I do think it's a good trigger to say,

00:58:17.750 --> 00:58:20.679
"How can I take this
experience that was given to me

00:58:20.679 --> 00:58:22.010
and regift it?

00:58:22.010 --> 00:58:23.958
Or also gift it?"

00:58:23.958 --> 00:58:25.818
'Cause I don't even have
to give up my experience

00:58:25.818 --> 00:58:26.778
to give another one.

00:58:26.778 --> 00:58:29.057
But it's interesting here,

00:58:29.057 --> 00:58:30.265
it doesn't say,

00:58:30.265 --> 00:58:32.515
"today make someone happy."

00:58:33.763 --> 00:58:37.671
He says, "today, invest in
someone else's happiness."

00:58:37.671 --> 00:58:38.504
Mm-hmm.

00:58:38.504 --> 00:58:42.337
I think a lot of times
there are people who,

00:58:45.100 --> 00:58:48.204
it would be a magical ability
to just make everybody happy.

00:58:48.204 --> 00:58:50.445
I mean, that's just the work of clowns.

00:58:52.195 --> 00:58:53.307
Some good clowns.

00:58:54.292 --> 00:58:56.435
I would say the small
percentage of clowns.

00:58:56.435 --> 00:58:58.069
Right.

00:58:58.069 --> 00:58:59.385
I'm sorry clowns.

00:58:59.385 --> 00:59:00.880
This is just the way it is.

00:59:00.880 --> 00:59:05.880
But an investment in to
me, it makes me think that,

00:59:06.043 --> 00:59:08.978
even if you can't make
someone happy in the moment,

00:59:08.978 --> 00:59:12.480
you can do something that
at least sow the seed

00:59:12.480 --> 00:59:16.488
of happiness down the road for them so.

00:59:16.488 --> 00:59:19.165
Well and also in the context of,

00:59:19.165 --> 00:59:20.737
I mean, he's got it in the phonebook,

00:59:20.737 --> 00:59:22.844
and we're talking about it
in the context with like,

00:59:22.844 --> 00:59:24.753
people that you interact with,

00:59:24.753 --> 00:59:26.225
acquaintances or whatever.

00:59:26.225 --> 00:59:28.472
But I also think about it in terms of

00:59:28.472 --> 00:59:30.672
your closest relationships
that you have, right so.

00:59:30.672 --> 00:59:31.505
Mm-hmm

00:59:31.505 --> 00:59:34.434
I think about the days that I get home.

00:59:34.434 --> 00:59:35.267
It's funny,

00:59:35.267 --> 00:59:36.613
I was thinking about getting home too,

00:59:36.613 --> 00:59:39.081
because we haven't been getting home.

00:59:39.081 --> 00:59:39.914
Right.

00:59:39.914 --> 00:59:40.747
'Cause we've been at home so much

00:59:40.747 --> 00:59:41.580
but we're starting to do that.

00:59:41.580 --> 00:59:42.743
Yeah, and so yeah.

00:59:42.743 --> 00:59:43.713
So I think about it more.

00:59:43.713 --> 00:59:46.963
I get home and I walk into the house,

00:59:48.072 --> 00:59:49.921
I see Jessie.

00:59:49.921 --> 00:59:52.588
And I think that a lot of times,

00:59:54.799 --> 00:59:57.882
my disposition is how is her presence

01:00:00.181 --> 01:00:02.191
or something that she says.

01:00:02.191 --> 01:00:05.604
Can she say or do something
that makes me okay

01:00:05.604 --> 01:00:07.411
with something that
happened to me today, right?

01:00:07.411 --> 01:00:09.492
Like I'm coming into the house,

01:00:09.492 --> 01:00:12.263
thinking about what I can get from her.

01:00:12.263 --> 01:00:14.335
Part of it is the fact that
Barbara greets me at the door,

01:00:14.335 --> 01:00:17.074
and Barbara is all about
what she can give to me.

01:00:17.074 --> 01:00:17.907
You're too invest...

01:00:17.907 --> 01:00:20.131
Yeah, you're willing to
receive an investment

01:00:20.131 --> 01:00:20.964
in your happiness.

01:00:20.964 --> 01:00:22.790
But if I went in and my mentality was

01:00:22.790 --> 01:00:25.540
regardless of how bad my day was,

01:00:27.930 --> 01:00:31.097
I'm going in with the mindset of like,

01:00:32.040 --> 01:00:33.541
invest in her happiness.

01:00:33.541 --> 01:00:37.909
And again, you can actually
do it for selfish reasons,

01:00:37.909 --> 01:00:40.816
just to be completely transparent.

01:00:40.816 --> 01:00:42.516
Because ultimately the
weird thing about this is,

01:00:42.516 --> 01:00:44.000
you kind of go full circle,

01:00:44.000 --> 01:00:44.833
and it's just like,

01:00:44.833 --> 01:00:46.119
this will make you happier.

01:00:46.119 --> 01:00:47.890
I think that's the beauty of it.

01:00:47.890 --> 01:00:48.723
Yeah.
I don't think that's the--

01:00:48.723 --> 01:00:49.863
That's not the motivation,

01:00:49.863 --> 01:00:50.696
I'm just saying.

01:00:50.696 --> 01:00:52.077
But the net result is,

01:00:52.077 --> 01:00:55.182
if you go in with that, surprise surprise,

01:00:55.182 --> 01:00:58.418
you will end up being happier ultimately.

01:00:58.418 --> 01:01:01.094
Yeah, and I think because
we are getting out more

01:01:01.094 --> 01:01:02.927
than our families are,

01:01:03.901 --> 01:01:08.151
we're in here connecting,
having this conversation,

01:01:09.099 --> 01:01:12.445
this is an investment
in our happiness, right?

01:01:12.445 --> 01:01:13.278
Mm-hmm.

01:01:14.270 --> 01:01:16.096
We're getting built up in a way.

01:01:16.096 --> 01:01:18.282
They're not leaving the
house as much as we are.

01:01:18.282 --> 01:01:21.865
So when we when we go
back to our families,

01:01:24.796 --> 01:01:28.787
I'm bringing an energy
that is hard to just create

01:01:28.787 --> 01:01:30.419
when you're at home all the time.

01:01:30.419 --> 01:01:31.252
Yeah.

01:01:31.252 --> 01:01:34.034
So starting to have more of
that mentality I think itself.

