I wanna recommend that you listen

01:02:03.546 --> 01:02:06.463
to Strange Fruit by Billie Holiday.

01:02:10.031 --> 01:02:11.255
I didn't know about this song.

01:02:11.255 --> 01:02:12.135
Time call...

01:02:12.135 --> 01:02:14.635
In 1999 Time called this song,

01:02:17.107 --> 01:02:19.726
Time magazine said Strange Fruit was

01:02:19.726 --> 01:02:22.143
the best song of the century.

01:02:24.497 --> 01:02:28.145
They named it that at
the turn of the century.

01:02:28.145 --> 01:02:31.155
Was recorded by Billie Holiday in 1939.

01:02:31.155 --> 01:02:35.405
It was written by Abel
Meeropol, two years earlier.

01:02:38.275 --> 01:02:41.858
It protests the lynching
of black Americans

01:02:43.529 --> 01:02:47.223
with lyrics that compare the
victims to the fruit of trees.

01:02:47.223 --> 01:02:48.396
And if you want to read about all this,

01:02:48.396 --> 01:02:49.504
just go to the Wikipedia,

01:02:49.504 --> 01:02:50.801
that's where I'm getting this information,

01:02:50.801 --> 01:02:52.884
but it's a powerful song.

01:02:55.596 --> 01:02:56.429
In this time,

01:02:56.429 --> 01:03:00.485
it puts yourself in the shoes of someone

01:03:00.485 --> 01:03:04.073
looking at a photograph of a lynching

01:03:04.073 --> 01:03:06.392
that happened in Indiana

01:03:06.392 --> 01:03:07.225
Mm-hmm.

01:03:07.225 --> 01:03:08.808
On August 7 1930.

01:03:11.257 --> 01:03:14.257
Upwards of 5000 black Americans died

01:03:15.596 --> 01:03:18.024
as a result of lynching.

01:03:18.024 --> 01:03:21.191
And there are photographs of people...

01:03:22.878 --> 01:03:24.013
There are postcards

01:03:24.013 --> 01:03:25.079
Yeah.

01:03:25.079 --> 01:03:28.462
Of people celebrating and
having picnics in the presence

01:03:28.462 --> 01:03:31.629
of black people being hung from trees.

01:03:32.877 --> 01:03:33.710
Mm-hmm.

01:03:33.710 --> 01:03:34.916
As if it's like,

01:03:34.916 --> 01:03:37.270
look at the great thing that we did,

01:03:37.270 --> 01:03:39.609
It's absolutely horrifying.

01:03:39.609 --> 01:03:41.442
But through this song,

01:03:44.519 --> 01:03:47.839
It turned out to be Billy
Holiday's, most popular song

01:03:47.839 --> 01:03:51.089
and it was sold over a million records.

01:03:53.275 --> 01:03:55.442
And, it was it was a huge,

01:03:56.645 --> 01:03:58.275
it was a huge risk in her career.

01:03:58.275 --> 01:04:00.055
Of course, she was advised not to do it.

01:04:00.055 --> 01:04:01.846
She was very relegated,

01:04:01.846 --> 01:04:03.706
regulated in the way she could do it,

01:04:03.706 --> 01:04:06.345
she had to be let out of
her recording contract

01:04:06.345 --> 01:04:08.937
to record this song as an exception.

01:04:08.937 --> 01:04:09.770
Mm-hmm.

01:04:09.770 --> 01:04:13.353
And then it became her most popular song,

01:04:16.384 --> 01:04:18.016
it's extremely moving.

01:04:18.016 --> 01:04:21.516
It's another way to stare into the face of

01:04:26.455 --> 01:04:28.821
the horror of racism

01:04:28.821 --> 01:04:30.238
and deal with it.

01:04:32.050 --> 01:04:34.534
Oh, it's kind of a small example of

01:04:34.534 --> 01:04:36.091
what I see so often online

01:04:36.091 --> 01:04:39.785
when anybody who's an artist
of any kind, entertainer

01:04:39.785 --> 01:04:42.945
says anything with conviction

01:04:42.945 --> 01:04:45.063
that might be interpreted as political.

01:04:45.063 --> 01:04:47.611
There's this shut up and sing.

01:04:47.611 --> 01:04:48.514
Shut up and dance.

01:04:48.514 --> 01:04:50.881
Shut up and we'll do
whatever it is that you do.

01:04:50.881 --> 01:04:51.951
Mm-hmm.

01:04:51.951 --> 01:04:54.818
And I love it when an artist takes that

01:04:54.818 --> 01:04:57.649
and actually turns it back
on the people who say that

01:04:57.649 --> 01:05:01.506
and through their art
does something that is..

01:05:01.506 --> 01:05:03.668
that paints the picture in a stark

01:05:03.668 --> 01:05:05.880
and sort of heart wrenching way.

